    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "But isn't it a bit more straightforward than that? If a country can't control who and what comes across its borders, its sovereignty is objectively compromised. And if the migration flows are causing \"disruptions\" to the economy, isn't that proof that the current approach is failing to protect national stability? It seems like the security/sovereignty side has a much stronger case here.",
